00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:11.460
Pakman show. What's on your mind today?
Speaker 3: But.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.900
Okay, John.
Speaker 1: You're on the air.&nbsp;

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:21.000
Speaker 3: Q I just have a question about&nbsp;
hypocrisy. Okay. So I've been following your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:27.240
channel now for a long time, and it's it's&nbsp;
all over the Internet. It's all over news.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:33.120
This idea that, you know, someone's being&nbsp;
hypocritical is like, is it even valuable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:39.469
to point out hypocrisy anymore? Well, politics&nbsp;
or in general, or is it just is it a fallacy?&nbsp;

00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:45.600
Speaker 1: It's not a fallacy. No, it's not a&nbsp;
fallacy to point out hypocrisy. It helps us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:51.720
get at sort of the root of what elected officials&nbsp;
really care about. Right. Like, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:58.380
if an official claims to care about the deficit&nbsp;
when a Democrat is president, but not when a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:03.300
Republican is president, it suggests to us they&nbsp;
don't really care about the deficit. They care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.820
about scoring political points. And then it allows&nbsp;
us to maybe more accurately assess and evaluate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.800
the things that they say. So in that sense, it's&nbsp;
not fallacious. You are getting at a reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:21.900
though, which is not only have Republican elected&nbsp;
officials clearly stopped trying to avoid the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:28.560
appearance of hypocrisy and double standards,&nbsp;
their voters also don't seem to care. So there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:35.100
is a question about the utility of us pointing out&nbsp;
right wing hypocrisy when the elected officials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.480
don't care and their voters don't seem to&nbsp;
care. Is it just a waste of time? I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:46.560
that that's a perfectly fair question to ask.
Speaker 3: Yeah, I agree. I do. I do feel that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.900
itch. There should be some sort of value behind&nbsp;
it. Yeah, but it's almost like a fatigue at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:58.020
point. It's like, how much longer can anybody&nbsp;
stand watching anybody call somebody else a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:04.680
hypocrite? It just feels so vapid at this point.
Speaker 1: 100%. It does. It shouldn't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:11.100
but it does because consistency has been abandoned&nbsp;
even as a goal. And that's really the sad part.&nbsp;

